# Every text file is checked out with LF endings, on every platform.
#
# Without this, git converts line endings according to each machine's `core.autocrlf`, so a
# Windows checkout of the repository has CRLF where a Linux checkout has LF. Spotless takes its
# line endings from git, so the two checkouts then disagree about what a formatted file is
# supposed to look like, and the Windows build reports files as unformatted that the Linux build
# accepts.
#
# `text=auto` leaves the binary files — images, jars, the Gradle wrapper — alone, and git still
# detects them the way it always has.
* text=auto eol=lf

# `cmd.exe` needs CRLF: a batch file with LF endings misbehaves.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
